  ],
  "text": "Computers do not use binary because it is elegant. They use it because on or off is the only thing you can trust. A wire does not carry a number. It carries a voltage, and voltage is messy. It sags over distance, drifts with heat, and picks up noise from everything nearby. Try to read ten different levels off one wire and you would misread constantly. Two levels leaves an enormous gap in the middle. Anything low is nought, anything high is one. A signal can wander a long way and still land unmistakably on one side of that gap. So binary is not simplicity. It is a deliberate trade of density for reliability. And two states is enough for anything at all, provided you have enough of them. Numbers, letters, the colour of a pixel, a moment of sound. All of it is just enough switches. The whole thing is built on the least ambiguous question you can ask. Is it on? One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
